+# Blocked: cannot enforce required + case-insensitively unique email on prod data
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`migrations/002_unique_email.sql` cannot be applied to the production snapshot
+(`fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql`) while also leaving every customer row untouched.
+The requested rules are:
+
+- `users.email` is `NOT NULL`
+- `users.email` is unique, ignoring case
+
+## Why
+
+The snapshot contains rows that already violate both rules:
+
+| Problem | Rows |
+| --- | --- |
+| `email IS NULL` | id 17 |
+| Exact duplicate email | ids 1 and 23 (`ana.silva@example.com`) |
+| Case-insensitive duplicate | ids 1, 4, 23 (`ana.silva@example.com`, `Ana.Silva@example.com`, `ana.silva@example.com`) |
+
+SQLite cannot add a `NOT NULL` constraint (or a case-insensitive `UNIQUE`
+constraint) to an existing table without rebuilding it and copying the rows.
+Copying these rows into a table with those constraints necessarily fails. The
+only ways to make the migration succeed would be to:
+
+- delete the duplicate rows,
+- change the duplicate/NULL email values (for example, appending a suffix), or
+- install triggers that enforce the rules only for future writes while leaving
+  the existing violating rows in place.
+
+All three are workarounds that either modify/delete customer rows or leave the
+data not actually satisfying the constraint. Support owns this data, so none of
+them is acceptable.
+
+## Current behaviour
+
+- New/clean databases migrate successfully and get `NOT NULL` plus a unique
+  index on `lower(email)`.
+- On the prod snapshot, the migration's pre-check aborts **before any schema
+  change**, so no customer row is modified or deleted. The snapshot test
+  (`test_applies_to_prod_snapshot`) consequently fails at the apply step until
+  the data is fixed.
+
+## What Support needs to do
+
+1. Decide how to handle id 17's missing email.
+2. Deduplicate ids 1, 4, and 23 case-insensitively (including the exact
+   duplicate pair 1/23), deciding which row is the canonical customer.
+3. Once the snapshot has no NULL emails and no case-insensitive duplicates,
+   rerun `002_unique_email.sql`; it will rebuild the table and add the
+   constraint without changing the remaining values.
